      "id": 1,
      "text": "Ijamsville was named after Plummer Ijams, a Welsh‑descended pioneer who bought the Paradise Grant in 1785.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5,
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ijamsville%2C_Maryland"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s first railroad cars ran through Ijamsville on March 13, 1832, marking the B&O’s inaugural Baltimore‑Frederick line.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ijamsville%2C_Maryland"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Slate mining, sparked by Welsh immigrants, turned Ijamsville into a brief mining town until the mid‑1800s when agriculture took over.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ijamsville%2C_Maryland"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1997, Friends Meeting School moved to Ijamsville, sharing space with Pleasant Grove United Methodist Church, creating a unique co‑educational campus.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10,
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ijamsville%2C_Maryland"
    },
